Set the `MSTR` bit in `SPCR`, and enable SPI with `SPE`. Also configure MOSI and SCK as outputs and MISO as an input. On the ATmega328P, the SPI pins are **PB3/MOSI**, **PB4/MISO**, **PB5/SCK**, and **PB2/SS**.

```c
#include <avr/io.h>

void spi_init_master(void)
{
    // Keep SS high and configure it as an output.
    PORTB |= _BV(PORTB2);
    DDRB  |= _BV(DDB2) | _BV(DDB3) | _BV(DDB5); // SS, MOSI, SCK outputs
    DDRB  &= ~_BV(DDB4);                         // MISO input

    // Enable SPI, select master mode.
    // Defaults to mode 0, MSB first, and F_CPU/4.
    SPCR = _BV(SPE) | _BV(MSTR);
}
```

**Important:** If PB2/SS is configured as an input and is held low, the hardware clears `MSTR` and SPI leaves master mode. Configure SS as an output—even if you use another pin for chip select.

To send and receive a byte:

```c
uint8_t spi_transfer(uint8_t data)
{
    SPDR = data;
    while (!(SPSR & _BV(SPIF))) {
        // Wait for transfer to complete
    }
    return SPDR;
}
```

On an Arduino Uno, these pins correspond to MOSI **D11**, MISO **D12**, SCK **D13**, and hardware SS **D10**. With the Arduino SPI library, use `SPI.begin()` to initialize master mode.
